  "purpose": "To read the Song first as love poetry, distinguish the poem’s plain sense from later Jewish and Christian devotional readings, recognize speaker and image ambiguities, reject coercive or purity-culture misuse, honor singleness and diverse life circumstances, and develop a Christ-shaped ethic of love that never confuses covenant with ownership.",
  "background": "Song of Songs is a collection or sequence of Hebrew love poems. Speaker labels found in modern Bibles are editorial aids, not part of the Hebrew consonantal text, and interpreters sometimes disagree about who speaks particular lines. The poetry moves through longing, praise, invitation, memory, absence, search, public celebration, conflict, reunion, and commitment rather than giving a simple chronological biography.\n\nJewish and Christian traditions have often read the Song allegorically or typologically as a picture of God’s love for Israel or Christ’s love for the church. Those readings can function devotionally within the canon, but the book’s direct literary voice celebrates human love. Faithful Christian interpretation should preserve that plain sense and then make canonical connections without turning every body part, spice, garden, or geographic image into a secret code.",
